 of Sines:\n\nAC / sin \u03b2 = BC / sin \u03b1\n\nIf BC = 1, then AC = sin 32.5\u00b0 / sin 62.5\u00b0 \u2248 0.536 / 0.887 \u2248 0.604. CD = AC \u2248 0.604. In triangle ACD, sides AC = CD \u2248 0.604, angle at C is 85\u00b0, so by Law of Sines:\n\nAD / sin 85\u00b0 = AC / sin(\u03b2 + 10\u00b0) => AD \u2248 (0.604 * sin 85\u00b0) / sin 42.5\u00b0 \u2248 (0.604 * 0.996) / 0.676 \u2248 0.899. Not sure if this is conflicting, but since the problem doesn't specify side lengths, this might be possible. But in this case, angle BAD = 20\u00b0 would be possible, but we need to check if the geometric construction holds.\n\nWait, but angle at C in triangle ACD is 85\u00b0, which is the same as angle \u03b3 in triangle ABC. If D is on the extension of BC beyond C, then angle ACD should be 180\u00b0 - \u03b3. But \u03b3 is 85\u00b0, so angle ACD should be 95\u00b0, but we have angle at C in triangle ACD as 85\u00b0, which contradicts. Therefore, this is impossible, so x = 20\u00b0 is invalid.\n\nSimilarly, testing x = 22.5\u00b0, angle CAD = \u03b2 + 7.5\u00b0, angle at C in ACD: 180\u00b0 - 2*(\u03b2 + 7.5\u00b0) = 165\u00b0 - 2\u03b2. Set equal to 30\u00b0 + 2\u03b2:\n\n165\u00b0 - 2\u03b2 = 30\u00b0 + 2\u03b2\n\n165\u00b0 - 30\u00b0 = 4\u03b2\n\n135\u00b0 = 4\u03b2 => \u03b2 = 33.75\u00b0, then \u03b1 = 63.75\u00b0, \u03b3 = 150\u00b0 - 67.5\u00b0 = 82.5\u00b0. Then angle at C in ACD should be 180\u00b0 - \u03b3 = 97.5\u00b0, but according to calculation, angle at C in ACD is 165\u00b0 - 2\u03b2 = 165\u00b0 - 67.5\u00b0 = 97.5\u00b0, which matches. So this works.\n\nWait, but in this case, angle BAD = 22.5\u00b0, which is option C. So is this possible?\n\nWait, so if \u03b2 = 33.75\u00b0, \u03b1 = 63.75\u00b0, \u03b3 = 82.5\u00b0, then angle at C in ACD is 97.5\u00b0, which is 180\u00b0 - \u03b3 = 180\u00b0 - 82.5\u00b0 = 97.5\u00b0, so that works. Then, angle CAD = \u03b2 + 7.5\u00b0 = 33.75\u00b0 + 7.5\u00b0 = 41.25\u00b0, angles at A and D in triangle ACD are 41.25\u