's compute:\n\nFor n=3, r=2:\n\n(n +1)*C(n, r) =4 * 3=12\n\nr * C(n +1, r +1)=2*C(4,3)=2*4=8\n\nSo 12 -8=4. Correct. But when I expressed in terms of factorials:\n\n(n +1)! / ( r! (n - r)! ) =4! / (2! 1! )=24 /2=12\n\nr * C(n +1, r +1)=2*(4! / (3!1! ))=2*(24/6)=2*4=8\n\nYes, so indeed 12 -8=4. So the expression S=(n +1)*C(n, r) - r*C(n +1, r +1) works. However, in general, when I expressed in terms of factorial:\n\n(n +1)*C(n, r) = (n +1)! / (r! (n -r)! )\n\nr*C(n +1, r +1)=r*( (n +1)! / ( (r +1)! (n -r)! )) )= (n +1)! / ( (r +1 -1)! (n -r)! )) = (n +1)! / (r! (n -r)! )\n\nTherefore, both terms are equal, but in reality, they are not. Wait, this is a contradiction. Wait, no. Wait, for n=3, r=2:\n\n(n +1)! / (r! (n -r)! ) =24/(2*1)=12\n\nr*C(n +1, r +1)=2*C(4,3)=8\n\nBut according to the general formula, (n +1)! / (r! (n -r)! ) = r*C(n +1, r +1). But in reality, 12 \u22608. So why is this happening?\n\nAh! Wait, my mistake. The general formula:\n\nC(n +1, r +1) = C(n, r +1) + C(n, r). Therefore, r*C(n +1, r +1)= r*C(n, r +1) + r*C(n, r). But (n +1)*C(n, r) is separate.\n\nWait, the key issue is that when I derived the identity sum_{m= r -1}^{n -1} m * C(m, r -1) = r*C(n +1, r +1) - C(n, r), this must be correct, but when translating into factorial terms, I must have made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let's compute S = (n +1)*C(n, r) - r*C(n +1, r +1).\n\nExpress in terms of C(n, r):\n\nC(n +1, r +1) = C(n, r +1) + C(n, r). Therefore,\n\nS = (n +1)*C(n, r) - r*(C(n, r +1) + C(n, r)) = (n +1 - r)*C(n, r) - r*C(n, r +1).\n\nBut how does this help? Let's compute this expression.\n\n(n +1 - r)*C(n, r) = (n +1 - r)*n! / (r! (n -r)! )\n\nr*C(n, r +1) = r* n! / ( (r +1)! (n -r -1)! )) = n! / ( (r)! (n -r -1)! )) \n\nSo S = [ (n +1 - r)*n! / (r! (n -r)! ) ] - [ n! / (r! (n -r -1)! ) ]\n\nFactor out n! / (r! (n -r -1)! ):\n\n= [n! / (r! (n -r -1)! ) ] * [ (n +1 - r)/ (n -r) -1 ]\n\nSimplify the term in brackets:\n\n(n +1 - r)/(n -r) -1 = [ (n +1 - r) - (n -r) ] / (n -r) = 1 / (n 