ent in a randomly chosen \\( r \\)-element subset. The goal is to compute \\( \\mathbb{E}[X] \\).\n\n2. **Probability that \\( X \\geq k \\):**\n   For any \\( k \\), \\( \\mathbb{P}(X \\geq k) \\) is the probability that all elements in the subset are at least \\( k \\). The number of such subsets is \\( \\binom{n - k + 1}{r} \\), chosen from \\( \\{k, k+1, \\ldots, n\\} \\). Thus,\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb{P}(X \\geq k) = \\frac{\\binom{n - k + 1}{r}}{\\binom{n}{r}}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Expectation via Cumulative Probabilities:**\n   The expectation can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb{E}[X] = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(X \\geq k).\n   \\]\n   Substituting the probability:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb{E}[X] = \\frac{1}{\\binom{n}{r}} \\sum_{k=1}^n \\binom{n - k + 1}{r}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Change of Variable:**\n   Let \\( m = n - k + 1 \\). When \\( k = 1 \\), \\( m = n \\); when \\( k = n \\), \\( m = 1 \\). The sum becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{m=1}^n \\binom{m}{r}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combinatorial Identity:**\n   The sum of binomial coefficients \\( \\sum_{m=r}^n \\binom{m}{r} = \\binom{n + 1}{r + 1} \\). Since \\( \\binom{m}{r} = 0 \\) for \\( m < r \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{m=1}^n \\binom{m}{r} = \\binom{n + 1}{r + 1}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Final Computation:**\n   Substitute this result into the expectation:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb{E}[X] = \\frac{\\binom{n + 1}{r + 1}}{\\binom{n}{r}} = \\frac{\\frac{(n + 1)!}{(r + 1)!(n - r)!}}{\\frac{n!}{r!(n - r)!}} = \\frac{n + 1}{r + 1}.\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe arithmetic mean \\( F(n, r) \\) of the smallest elements in all \\( r \\)-element subsets is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{n + 1}{r + 1}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo show that \n\\[ F(n, r) = \\frac{n+1}{r+1}, \\]\nwhere $F(n, r)$ is the arithmetic average of the smallest numbers in all $r$-element subsets of $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Counting Subsets:**\n   The number of $r$-element subsets of $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$ is given by the binomial coefficient \n   \\[ C_n^r = \\binom{n}{r}. \\]\n\n2. **Minimum Element Analysis:**\n   If one $r$-element subset's smallest element is $j$, the remaining $r-1$ elements must come from $\\{j+1, \\ldots, n\\}$. \n   Therefore, the number of such $r$-element subsets with their smallest element as $j$ is \n   \\[ C_{n-j}^{r-1} = \\binom{n-j}{r-1}. \\]\n\n3. **Summation Formula:**\n   Summing over all possible values of the smallest element $j$ from 1 to $n-r+1$, we get:\n   \\[ \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} C_{n-j}^{r-1} = \\binom{n}{r}. \\]\n\n4. **Total Sum of Minimum Values:**\n   Let's denote the sum of the smallest elements of all $r$-element subsets of $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$ by $S$. Then,\n   \\[ S = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} j \\binom{n-j}{r-1}. \\]\n\n5. **Rewriting and Simplifying the Sum:**\n   We can rewrite $S$ as follows:\n   \\[ \n   S = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} j \\binom{n-j}{r-1} \n   = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} j \\binom{n-j}{r-1} \n   = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} \\left( j \\binom{n-j}{r-1} \\right). \n   \\]\n\n   By splitting the sum and simplifying:\n   \\[\n   S = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} \\left( (j-1+1) \\binom{n-j}{r-1} \\right) \n   = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} \\binom{n-j}{r-1} + \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} (j-1) \\binom{n-j}{r-1}. \n   \\]\n\n   The first sum is:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{n-r+1} \\binom{n-j}{r-1} = \\binom{n}{r}.\n   \\]\n\n   For the second sum, recognize the sum shifted index:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=2}^{n-r+1} (j-1) \\binom{n-j}{r-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Reindex by setting $k=j-1$:\n   \\[\n   = \\sum_{k=1}^{n-r} k \\binom{n-1-k}{r-1} = \\binom{n-1}{r}.\n   \\]\n\n   Continuing this reindexing summation process:\n   \\[\n   S = \\binom{n}{r} + \\binom{n-1}{r} + \\sum_{k=2}^{n-r} (k-1) \\binom{n-k}{r-1}\n   = \\cdots = \\binom{n+1}{r+1}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculating $F(n, r)$:**\n   The average of all the smallest elements is given by:\n   \\[\n   F(n, r) = \\frac{S}{C_{n}^{r}} = \\frac{\\binom{n+1}{r+1}}{\\binom{n}{r}}.\n   \\]\n\n   Using properties of binomial coefficients:\n   \\[\n 