\lfloor \\frac{x}{10} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{11} \\right\\rfloor + 1\n\\]\n\n2. **Let's Express $x$ in Terms of $11n + r$**:\n   Assume \\( x = 11n + r \\), where \\( n \\geq 0 \\) and \\( 0 \\leq r \\leq 10 \\).\n\n   Then,\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{11} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11n + r}{11} \\right\\rfloor = n\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute into the Given Equation**:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{10} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11n + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11n}{10} + \\frac{r}{10} \\right\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   We know that:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11n + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11n}{10} + \\frac{r}{10} \\right\\rfloor = n + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{10} + \\frac{r}{10} \\right\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   Given:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11n + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor = n + 1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the Equation**:\n   \\[\n   n + 1 = n + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n5. **Isolate and Simplify the Floor Function**:\n   \\[\n   1 = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n + r}{10} \\right\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   10 \\leq n + r < 20\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find the Bounds for $n$**:\n   \\[\n   10 \\leq n + r < 20\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   10 - r \\leq n < 20 - r\n   \\]\n\n7. **Counting Solutions for Each \\( r \\)**:\n   For each \\( r \\) in the range \\( 0 \\leq r \\leq 10 \\), we need to count the number of integers \\( n \\) satisfying \\( 10 - r \\leq n < 20 - r \\).\n\n   Let's calculate this for each \\( r \\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   r = 0 & : 10 \\leq n < 20 & \\rightarrow  & \\text{ 10 values: } 10, 11, 12, \\ldots, 19, \\\\\n   r = 1 & : 9 \\leq n < 19 & \\rightarrow  & \\text{ 10 values: } 9, 10, 11, \\ldots, 18, \\\\\n   & \\vdots \\\\\n   r = 10 & : 0 \\leq n < 10 & \\rightarrow  & \\text{ 10 values: } 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9. \\\\\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\( r \\) from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 10 \\