ied by 2/3? Wait, let's check.\n\nIf the triangle is equilateral with side length a, then the altitude h = (sqrt(3)/2) a. The inradius r = h / 3 = (sqrt(3)/6) a. The maximum point here would have distances d_a = d_b = d_c = (2 Area)/(3 a). The area of the equilateral triangle is (sqrt(3)/4) a\u00b2. Therefore, 2 Area = (sqrt(3)/2) a\u00b2. Then, (2 Area)/(3 a) = (sqrt(3)/2 a\u00b2) / (3 a) ) = (sqrt(3)/6) a, which is exactly equal to the inradius. So in this case, the maximum occurs at the incenter. Interesting.\n\nBut in a general triangle, does the maximum occur at the incenter? Wait, no. Wait, in the general case, the distances we found are (2 Area)/(3 a), (2 Area)/(3 b), (2 Area)/(3 c). The inradius is 2 Area / (a + b + c). So unless (2 Area)/(3 a) = 2 Area / (a + b + c), which would require 1/(3a) = 1/(a + b + c), or a + b + c = 3a, i.e., b + c = 2a. Which is only true in a specific triangle, not in general. So in general, the distances we found are different from the inradius.\n\nTherefore, the point that maximizes the product is not the incenter. So, what is this point?\n\nFrom the Lagrangian solution, we found that the distances are proportional to 1/a, 1/b, 1/c. So the coordinates of this point can be found in terms of barycentric coordinates? Let me recall that in barycentric coordinates, the weights are proportional to the areas.\n\nWait, barycentric coordinates are typically given as masses proportional to the areas opposite the vertices. But maybe we need to relate the distances here to barycentric coordinates. Alternatively, think in terms of trilinear coordinates.\n\nTrilinear coordinates represent a point as proportional to the distances to the sides. So, if the distances are d_a, d_b, d_c, then the trilinear coordinates are d_a : d_b : d_c. From the Lagrangian result, we found that d_a : d_b : d_c = 1/a : 1/b : 1/c. Therefore, the trilinear coordinates of the maximum product point are proportional to 1/a : 1/b : 1/c.\n\nBut what point has trilinear 