{s}{t-1}}\n   \\]\n\n9. We have two cases to consider:\n   - Case (a): \\(y \\geq t\\):\n     Prove the function \\( f_1(\\alpha) = \\alpha^{\\alpha / (\\alpha-1)} \\) is monotonically increasing for \\(\\alpha > 1\\). The derivative is:\n     \\[\n     f_1'(\\alpha) = \\alpha^{\\alpha / (\\alpha-1)} \\frac{1}{(\\alpha-1)^2} (\\alpha-1 - \\ln \\alpha) > 0\n     \\]\n     Because \\(g(\\alpha) = \\alpha - 1 - \\ln \\alpha > 0\\) for \\(\\alpha > 1\\).\n\n     Thus:\n     \\[\n     f_1(y) \\geq f_1(t) \\implies y^{y / (y-1)} \\geq t^{t / (t-1)} \\geq t^{s / (t-1)}\n     \\]\n\n   - Case (b): \\(y < t\\):\n     Prove the function \\( f_2(\\alpha) = \\alpha^{1 / (\\alpha-1)} \\) is monotonically decreasing for \\(\\alpha > 1\\). The derivative is:\n     \\[\n     f_2'(\\alpha) = \\alpha^{1 / (\\alpha-1)} \\frac{1}{\\alpha (\\alpha-1)^2} (\\alpha-1 - \\alpha \\ln \\alpha) < 0\n     \\]\n     Because \\(g(\\alpha) = \\alpha - 1 - \\alpha \\ln \\alpha < 0\\) for \\(\\alpha > 1\\).\n\n     Thus:\n     \\[\n     (f_2(y))^y \\geq (f_2(t))^y \\implies t^{y / (t-1)} \\geq t^{s / (t-1)}\n     \\]\n\nHence, the inequality holds in both cases, proving the required inequality.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{a^b b^c c^d d^a \\geq b^a c^b d^c a^d}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the greatest number among \\(5^{100}\\), \\(6^{91}\\), \\(7^{90}\\), \\(8^{85}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out which of these numbers is the largest: 5^100, 6^91, 7^90, 8^85. Hmm, they all have different bases and exponents, so comparing them directly isn't straightforward. Let me think about how to approach this. Maybe I can use logarithms to compare their sizes because logarithms can help simplify the exponents. \n\nFirst, I remember that taking the natural logarithm of a number preserves the order because ln is a monotonically increasing function. So if I take ln(5^100) and compare it to ln(6^91), ln(7^9