e graph. The complement of our graph would have 15 - 9 = 6 edges. The complement of a triangle-free graph isn't necessarily anything specific, so I don't know if that helps.\n\nWait, another thought. In Ramsey theory, the Ramsey number R(3,3) is 6, meaning that in any 2-coloring of the edges of K\u2086, there is either a monochromatic triangle or a monochromatic triangle. Wait, but that might not directly apply here. However, it tells us that any graph on 6 vertices with enough edges will contain a triangle or its complement will. But our problem is not about coloring, but just about existence. Since we need a graph that doesn't contain a triangle, so the maximum edge count is just below the point where a triangle is forced. Ramsey number R(3,3)=6 means that any graph on 6 vertices will have either a triangle or an independent set of size 3. But our problem allows independent sets, we just need no triangles. So perhaps Tur\u00e1n's theorem is still the right approach.\n\nGiven all this, I think it's safe to conclude that the maximum number of edges is 9, achieved by the complete bipartite graph K_{3,3}, and that any attempt to add a 10th edge would necessarily create a triangle. Therefore, the answer should be 9.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe maximum number of line segments that can be drawn is \\boxed{9}.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the maximum number of line segments that can be drawn between 6 points on a plane such that no three points form a triangle, we can use Tur\u00e1n's theorem. Tur\u00e1n's theorem states that the maximum number of edges in an \\( n \\)-vertex graph that does not contain a complete subgraph of order \\( r + 1 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Tur\u00e1n number } T(n, r) = \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{r}\\right) \\frac{n^2}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor our problem, we need a triangle-free graph (i.e., avoiding \\( K_3 \\)), which corresponds to \\( r = 2 \\). Plugging in \\( n = 6 \\):\n\n\\[\nT(6, 2) = \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\frac{6^2}{2} = \\frac{