erable. Let me verify that 2 sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1 is indeed equal to sin 2\u03b1. Yes, the double-angle identity: sin 2\u03b1 = 2 sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1. Therefore, the simplified expression is sin 2\u03b1. \n\nTherefore, the final simplified form of the original expression is sin 2\u03b1.\n\nWait, but let me check again if all the steps are correct because sometimes there can be a miscalculation. Let me re-examine:\n\nStarting with the original expression:\n\nsin\u00b2\u03b1 [ (1 + csc\u03b1 + cot\u03b1)(1 - csc\u03b1 + cot\u03b1) ]\n\nMultiply the two binomials:\n\n= sin\u00b2\u03b1 [ (1 + cot\u03b1 + csc\u03b1)(1 + cot\u03b1 - csc\u03b1) ]\n\nWhich is of the form (A + B)(A - B) where A = 1 + cot\u03b1, B = csc\u03b1\n\n= sin\u00b2\u03b1 [ (1 + cot\u03b1)^2 - (csc\u03b1)^2 ]\n\nExpanding (1 + cot\u03b1)^2:\n\n= 1 + 2 cot\u03b1 + cot\u00b2\u03b1\n\nThus:\n\nsin\u00b2\u03b1 [ 1 + 2 cot\u03b1 + cot\u00b2\u03b1 - csc\u00b2\u03b1 ]\n\nBut since 1 + cot\u00b2\u03b1 = csc\u00b2\u03b1, replacing that:\n\n= sin\u00b2\u03b1 [ csc\u00b2\u03b1 + 2 cot\u03b1 - csc\u00b2\u03b1 ]\n\n= sin\u00b2\u03b1 [ 2 cot\u03b1 ]\n\n= 2 sin\u00b2\u03b1 cot\u03b1\n\nBut cot\u03b1 = cos\u03b1 / sin\u03b1, so:\n\n= 2 sin\u00b2\u03b1 * (cos\u03b1 / sin\u03b1) = 2 sin\u03b1 cos\u03b1 = sin2\u03b1\n\nYes, exactly. Therefore, the entire expression simplifies to sin2\u03b1. So, the answer is sin2\u03b1.\n\nAlternatively, to write it with the Greek letter, it's \\(\\sin 2\\alpha\\). So, boxed, it would be \\boxed{\\sin 2\\alpha}.\n\nI think that's correct. Let me just consider if there was any mistake in the assumption about the notation. If \\(\\sin^{-1}\\alpha\\) was intended to be arcsin(alpha), then the expression would be problematic because if alpha is an angle, then the domain of arcsin is between -1 and 1, but angles can take any real number, leading to undefined terms. Therefore, it's more plausible that \\(\\sin^{-1}\\alpha\\) is the reciprocal, i.e., cosecant. Therefore, the previous steps are valid.\n\nAlternatively, if by some chance, \\(\\sin^{-1}\\alpha\\) was intended to be the inverse function, then we would have an expression involving arcsin(alpha), which would complicate things, but since alpha is likely an angle (as it's common in trigonometric expressions), and arcsin(alpha) would require alpha to be a value between -1 and 1, which is not typically the case for an angle variable. Therefore, the initial interpretation as cosecant is correct.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that the expression simplifies to \\(\\sin 2\\alpha\\), which is the final answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\sin 2\\alpha}", "deepseek_solution": "To simplify the expression \\(\\sin ^{2} \\alpha\\left(1+\\sin ^{-1} \\alpha+\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha\\right)\\left(1-\\sin ^{-1} \\alpha+\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha\\right)\\), we start by interpreting \\(\\sin^{-1} \\alpha\\) as the reciprocal of \\(\\sin \\alpha\\) (i.e., \\(\\csc \\alpha\\)) and \\(\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha\\) as \\(\\cot \\alpha\\). \n\nRewriting the expression, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha \\left(1 + \\csc \\alpha + \\cot \\alpha \\right)\\left(1 - \\csc \\alpha + \\cot \\alpha \\right)\n\\]\n\nRecognizing the product of the form \\((A + B)(A - B)\\) where \\(A = 1 + \\cot \\alpha\\) and \\(B = \\csc \\alpha\\), we use the difference of squares:\n\\[\n(1 + \\cot \\alpha + \\csc \\alpha)(1 + \\cot \\alpha - \\csc \\alpha) = (1 + \\cot \\alpha)^2 - (\\csc \\alpha)^2\n\\]\n\nExpanding \\((1 + \\cot \\alpha)^2\\) and using the Pythagorean identity \\(1 + \\cot^2 \\alpha = \\csc^2 \\alpha\\):\n\\[\n(1 + \\cot \\alpha)^2 - \\csc^2 \\alpha = 1 + 2 \\cot \\alpha + \\cot^2 \\alpha - \\csc^2 \\alpha = 2 \\cot \\alpha\n\\]\n\nSubstituting back into the original expression:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha \\cdot 2 \\cot \\alpha\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(\\cot \\alpha = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\\):\n\\[\n2 \\sin^2 \\alpha \\cdot \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha\n\\]\n\nRecognizing the double-angle identity, we get:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha = \\sin 2\\alpha\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sin 2\\alpha}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Start with the given expression:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha \\left(1 + \\sin^{-1} \\alpha + \\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha \\right) \\left(1 - \\sin^{-1} \\alpha + \\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha \\right).\n\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(\\sin^{-1} \\alpha\\) by \\(\\frac{1}{\\sin \\alpha}\\) and \\( \\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha\\) by \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\):\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin \\alpha} + \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{\\sin \\alpha} + \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\right).\n\\]\n\n3. Simplify the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha \\left( \\frac{\\sin \\alpha + 1 + \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\right) \\left( \\frac{\\sin \\alpha - 1 + \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\right).\n\\]\n\n4. Notice that both terms have a common denominator, \\(\\sin \\alpha\\):\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha \\cdot \\frac{(\\sin \\alpha + 1 + \\cos \\alpha)(\\sin \\alpha - 1 + \\cos \\alpha)}{\\sin^2 \\alpha}.\n\\]\n\n5. Simplify the expression by canceling out \\(\\sin^2 \\alpha\\):\n\\[\n(\\sin \\alpha + 1 + \\cos \\alpha)(\\sin \\alpha - 1 + \\cos \\alpha).\n\\]\n\n6. Use the difference of squares formula \\( (a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\\), where \\(a = \\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\alpha\\) and \\(b = 1\\):\n\\[\n(\\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\alpha)^2 - 1^2.\n\\]\n\n7. Compute the squared term:\n\\[\n(\\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\alpha)^2 - 1.\n\\]\n\n8. Expand the squared term:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 \\alpha + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha - 1.\n\\]\n\n9. Use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha = 1\\):\n\\[\n1 + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha - 1.\n\\]\n\n10. Simplify the expression by canceling out the \\(1\\):\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha.\n\\]\n\n11. Recognize that \\(2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha\\) is the double-angle identity for sine:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha = \\sin 2 \\alpha.\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sin 2 \\alpha}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Write down and calculate all seven-digit numbers whose digit sum equals 2. How many such numbers are there?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all seven-digit numbers where the sum of the digits equals 2. Then I have to figure out how many there are. Let me start by understanding the problem properly.\n\nFirst, a seven-digit number means it's a number from 1,000,000 to 9,999,999. So the first digit can't be zero. All other digits can be from 0 to 9, but the sum of all seven digits has to be exactly 2. Hmm, right. So the first digit is at least 1, but the total sum is 2. That means the remaining six digits must sum to 1, because the first digit is already 1. Wait, but if the first digit is 1, then the sum of the other six digits has to be 1. But what if the first digit is 2? Then the sum of the remaining six digits would be 0. But a seven-digit number can't start with 0, so the first digit is at least 1. But if the first digit is 2, then the rest must all be zeros. Let me confirm.\n\nWait, the total digit sum is 2. So the possible cases for the first digit are either 1 or 2. Because if the first digit is 3 or higher, then even if the rest of the digits are zero, the sum would be 3 or more, which is over the total of 2. So the first digit can only be 1 or 2.\n\nCase 1: First digit is 1. Then the remaining six digits must sum to 1 (since 2 - 1 = 1). How many six-digit numbers (including leading zeros) have digits summing to 1? That's equivalent to the number of ways to distribute 1 \"unit\" among six digits, where each digit can be 0-9. But since the sum is only 1, each digit can be either 0 or 1, but only one of them is 1 and the rest are 0. So the number of such combinations is equal to the number of positions where the 1 can be placed. There are 6 digits, so 6 possibilities.\n\nCase 2: First digit is 2. Then the remaining six digits must sum to 0 (since 2 - 2 = 0). The only way this can happen is if all the remaining six digits are 0. So there's only 1 such number: 2000000.\n\nTherefore, the total number of seven-digit numbers with digit sum 2 is the sum of the two cases: 6 (from first case) + 1 (from second case) = 7.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Let's think of another approach. The problem is similar to the stars and bars theorem in combinatorics. We need to find the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation:\n\nd1 + d2 + d3 + d4 + d5 + d6 + d7 = 2,\n\nwhere d1 is the first digit (must be \u22651, since it's a seven-digit number), and d2 to d7 can be \u22650.\n\nBut because d1 \u22651, we can substitute d1' = d1 - 1, which makes d1' \u22650. Then the equation becomes:\n\nd1' + 1 + d2 + d3 + d4 + d5 + d6 + d7 = 2\n\nSimplifying:\n\nd1' + d2 + d3 + d4 + d5 + d6 + d7 = 1\n\nNow, this is the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation where seven variables sum to 1. The formula for this is C(1 + 7 - 1, 7 - 1) = C(7,6) = 7. So that's 7 solutions. But wait, this includes all possibilities where the original d1 could be 1 or 2. Because when we substituted d1' = d1 -1, d1' can be 0 or 1 (since original d1 can be 1 or 2). But in the transformed equation, d1' can be 0 or 1, and the rest can be 0. So the number of solutions is indeed 7. But according to the stars and bars formula, it's C(n + k -1, k -1), where n is the number to sum to, and k the number of variables. Here n=1, k=7, so C(1+7-1,7-1) = C(7,6)=7. So that matches my previous calculation. Therefore, there are 7 such numbers.\n\nBut let me list them out to confirm. If the first digit is 1, then one of the other digits is 1 and the rest are 0. So numbers like 1000001, 1000010, 1000100, 1001000, 1010000, 1100000. That's 6 numbers. Then the first digit is 2, and the rest are zeros: 2000000. So total 7 numbers. Yes, that's correct. Therefore, the answer is 7.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the number of seven-digit numbers with a digit sum of 2:\n\n1. **Constraints**: A seven-digit number starts with a digit \\( d_1 \\geq 1 \\), and the sum of all digits \\( d_1 + d_2 + \\dots + d_7 = 2 \\).\n\n2. **Case Analysis**:\n   - **Case 1**: \\( d_1 = 1 \\). The remaining six digits must sum to \\( 2 - 1 = 1 \\). This is equivalent to distributing 1 \"unit\" across six positions, which has \\( \\binom{6}{1} = 6 \\) solutions (e.g., 1000001, 1000010, ..., 1100000).\n   - **Case 2**: \\( d_1 = 2 \\). The remaining digits must sum to \\( 2 - 2 = 0 \\), meaning all are zero. There is exactly \\( 1 \\) such number: 2000000.\n\n3. **Total Count**: Combining both cases, there are \\( 6 + 1 = 7 \\) valid numbers.\n\n**Final Answer**: There are \\(\\boxed{7}\\) seven-digit numbers whose digits sum to 2.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find all seven-digit numbers where the sum of the digits is 2, we need to find the combinations of digits (0-9) that add up to 2 and ensure that there are exactly 7 digits in total. Each digit can only be 0 or greater, and the first digit must be 1 or 2 because it cannot be zero (as this would mean a six-digit or shorter number).\n\n1. **Identifying Possible Combinations:**\n    - The valid combinations of digits that sum to 2 and result in seven-digit numbers can include:\n      - One digit is 2 (rest are zeros).\n      - One digit is 1, another digit is 1 (rest are zeros).\n\n2. **Enumerating the numbers:**\n    - Let's place 2 in the first digit and the remaining digits being zeros:  \n      \\[\n      2000000\n      \\]\n    - Place 1 in the first digit, another 1 in the second digit, and the rest are zeros:  \n      \\[\n      1100000\n      \\]\n    - Place 1 in the first digit, another 1 in the third digit, and the rest are zeros:  \n      \\[\n      1010000\n      \\]\n    - Continue this process for all other placements of 1.\n    \n    Thus, the numbers are: \n    \\[\n    \\begin{align*}\n    2000000, \\\\\n    1100000, \\\\\n    1010000, \\\\\n    1001000, \\\\\n    1000100, \\\\\n    1000010, \\\\\n    1000001 \n    \\end{align*}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n    - We have listed all possible seven-digit combinations where the sum of the digits is 2. There are 7 such numbers.\n\n\\[ \\boxed{7} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If one side of square \\( ABCD \\) lies on the line \\( y = 2x - 17 \\) and the other two vertices are on the parabola \\( y = x^2 \\), what is the minimum possible area of the square?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's try to figure out this problem. So, we have a square ABCD where one side lies on the line y = 2x - 17, and the other two vertices are on the parabola y = x\u00b2. We need to find the minimum possible area of such a square. Hmm, okay.\n\nFirst, let me visualize this. There's a line with slope 2, which is fairly steep, and it's shifted down by 17 units. The square has one side on this line, meaning two vertices (let's say A and B) are somewhere on this line. The other two vertices, C and D, are on the parabola y = x\u00b2. So, the square is kind of \"standing\" on the line with two of its corners, and the other two corners are touching the parabola.\n\nSince it's a square, all sides are equal, and the sides are perpendicular to each other. The line y = 2x -17 has a slope of 2, so the side of the square lying on this line will also have the same slope. The adjacent sides of the square should be perpendicular to this line. The slope of a line perpendicular to another with slope m is -1/m, so here, the perpendicular slope would be -1/2. Therefore, the sides of the square not on the line y = 2x -17 will have a slope of -1/2.\n\nWait, but actually, in a square, adjacent sides are perpendicular. So, if one side is along a line with slope 2, then the adjacent sides should be along lines with slope -1/2, right? So, the direction from A to B is along the line y = 2x -17, and the direction from B to C is along a line with slope -1/2. Similarly, from A to D, also slope -1/2.\n\nBut how do the points C and D end up on the parabola? Let me try to parametrize this.\n\nLet's denote the two points on the line as A and B. Let's suppose that the coordinates of A are (a, 2a -17) and B are (b, 2b -17). Since AB is a side of the square, the length of AB is equal to the side length of the square. Also, the vectors AB and BC should be perpendicular and of equal length.\n\nThe vector AB is (b - a, 2(b - a)). Then, the vector BC should be perpendicular to AB, so their dot product should be zero. If vector BC is (c - b, d - (2b -17)), then:\n\n(b - a)(c - b) + 2(b - a)(d - 2b +17) = 0\n\nBut since BC is supposed to be a side of the square, it should also have the same length as AB, and direction perpendicular to AB. Since AB has a direction of (1, 2), then BC should have a direction of (-2, 1) or (2, -1), but scaled appropriately.\n\nWait, maybe there's a better way. If the side AB is along the line y = 2x -17, then the displacement from A to B is (h, 2h), where h is the horizontal difference. Because for every unit you move right, you go up 2 units. Then, the length of AB is sqrt(h\u00b2 + (2h)\u00b2) = sqrt(5h\u00b2) = h\u221a5.\n\nThe next side BC should be perpendicular to AB and of the same length. So, the displacement from B to C would be a vector perpendicular to (h, 2h). A perpendicular vector would be (-2h, h) or (2h, -h). Since the square can be on either side of the line, but in this case, the other two vertices are on the parabola y = x\u00b2, which is above the line y = 2x -17 (since x\u00b2 is always non-negative and 2x -17 can be negative or positive depending on x). Wait, but maybe not necessarily. Let me check.\n\nIf we take a point on the line y = 2x -17. For example, when x = 0, y = -17, which is way below the parabola y = x\u00b2. As x increases, y increases. The parabola y = x\u00b2 is a U-shaped curve opening upwards. So, the line y = 2x -17 will intersect the parabola at points where 2x -17 = x\u00b2, which is x\u00b2 -2x +17 =0. The discriminant is 4 - 68 = -64, which is negative, so the line does not intersect the parabola. Therefore, the entire line y = 2x -17 is below the parabola y = x\u00b2. Therefore, the square has two vertices on this line and the other two vertices above, on the parabola.\n\nTherefore, the displacement from B to C should be upwards, towards the parabola. So, the perpendicular vector should be (-2h, h) because (h, 2h) dot (-2h, h) = h*(-2h) + 2h*(h) = -2h\u00b2 + 2h\u00b2 = 0. So, yes, that's perpendicular. But if h is positive, then (-2h, h) would be going left and up, while (2h, -h) would be right and down. Since the parabola is above the line, we need to go up, so maybe the direction is (-2h, h). Let me verify.\n\nSuppose we have point B at (b, 2b -17). Then point C would be B + (-2h, h) = (b - 2h, 2b -17 + h). Similarly, point D would be A + (-2h, h) = (a - 2h, 2a -17 + h). But points C and D need to lie on the parabola y = x\u00b2. Therefore, their coordinates must satisfy y = x\u00b2.\n\nSo, for point C: 2b -17 + h = (b - 2h)\u00b2\n\nAnd for point D: 2a -17 + h = (a - 2h)\u00b2\n\nAdditionally, since AB is from A(a, 2a -17) to B(b, 2b -17), the vector AB is (b - a, 2(b - a)), which is equal to (h, 2h) if we let h = b - a. Therefore, h is the horizontal distance between A and B, and the length of AB is h\u221a5.\n\nTherefore, we can express b = a + h.\n\nSo, substituting b = a + h into the equation for point C:\n\n2(a + h) -17 + h = ( (a + h) - 2h )\u00b2\n\nSimplify left side: 2a + 2h -17 + h = 2a + 3h -17\n\nRight side: (a + h - 2h)\u00b2 = (a - h)\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - 2ah + h\u00b2\n\nSo equation becomes:\n\n2a + 3h -17 = a\u00b2 - 2ah + h\u00b2  ...(1)\n\nSimilarly, for point D:\n\n2a -17 + h = (a - 2h)^2\n\nLeft side: 2a -17 + h\n\nRight side: a\u00b2 -4ah +4h\u00b2\n\nEquation:\n\n2a -17 + h = a\u00b2 -4ah +4h\u00b2  ...(2)\n\nNow, we have two equations (1) and (2) with variables a and h. Let's write them out again:\n\nFrom (1):\n\na\u00b2 - 2ah + h\u00b2 -2a -3h +17 = 0\n\nFrom (2):\n\na\u00b2 -4ah +4h\u00b2 -2a -h +17 =0\n\nSo, let's denote equation (1) as:\n\nEquation (1): a\u00b2 -2ah + h\u00b2 -2a -3h +17 =0\n\nEquation (2): a\u00b2 -4ah +4h\u00b2 -2a -h +17 =0\n\nNow, subtract equation (1) from equation (2):\n\n(a\u00b2 -4ah +4h\u00b2 -2a -h +17) - (a\u00b2 -2ah + h\u00b2 -2a -3h +17) =0\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\na\u00b2 - a\u00b2 = 0\n\n-4ah - (-2ah) = -2ah\n\n4h\u00b2 - h\u00b2 = 3h\u00b2\n\n-2a - (-2a) =0\n\n-h - (-3h) = 2h\n\n17 -17 =0\n\nTherefore, combining:\n\n-2ah +3h\u00b2 +2h =0\n\nFactor out h:\n\nh(-2a +3h +2)=0\n\nSo, either h=0 or -2a +3h +2=0\n\nBut h=0 would mean the points A and B are the same, which can't be a square. So we discard h=0 and solve:\n\n-2a +3h +2=0 => 2a = 3h +2 => a = (3h +2)/2\n\nNow, substitute a = (3h +2)/2 into equation (1) or (2). Let's substitute into equation (1):\n\na\u00b2 -2ah + h\u00b2 -2a -3h +17 =0\n\nFirst compute a\u00b2:\n\n[(3h +2)/2]^2 = (9h\u00b2 +12h +4)/4\n\nNext term: -2ah\n\n= -2*(3h +2)/2 * h = - (3h +2)*h = -3h\u00b2 -2h\n\nNext term: +h\u00b2\n\nThen -2a:\n\n= -2*(3h +2)/2 = -(3h +2)\n\nThen -3h +17\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n(9h\u00b2 +12h +4)/4 -3h\u00b2 -2h + h\u00b2 -3h -2 -3h +17 =0\n\nLet me compute term by term:\n\nFirst term: (9h\u00b2 +12h +4)/4\n\nSecond term: -3h\u00b2\n\nThird term: -2h\n\nFourth term: +h\u00b2\n\nFifth term: -3h -2 (from -2a term)\n\nSixth term: -3h (from -3h term)\n\nSeventh term: +17\n\nSo combine like terms:\n\n(9h\u00b2/4 +12h/4 +4/4) + (-3h\u00b2 + h\u00b2) + (-2h -3h -3h) + (-2 +17)\n\nSimplify each part:\n\nFor h\u00b2 terms:\n\n9h\u00b2/4 + (-2h\u00b2) = 9h\u00b2/4 -8h\u00b2/4 = (9h\u00b2 -8h\u00b2)/4 = h\u00b2/4\n\nFor h terms:\n\n12h/4 + (-8h) = 3h -8h = -5h\n\nConstants:\n\n4/4 +15 =1 +15=16\n\nSo overall equation:\n\nh\u00b2/4 -5h +16 =0\n\nMultiply through by 4 to eliminate denominator:\n\nh\u00b2 -20h +64=0\n\nSolve quadratic equation:\n\nh = [20 \u00b1 sqrt(400 -256)]/2 = [20 \u00b1 sqrt(144)]/2 = [20 \u00b112]/2\n\nSo, h=(20+12)/2=32/2=16 or h=(20-12)/2=8/2=4\n\nTherefore, h=16 or h=4\n\nNow, check which one is valid.\n\nFirst, h=16:\n\nThen, a=(3*16 +2)/2=(48 +2)/2=50/2=25\n\nThen, b =a + h=25 +16=41\n\nCheck point C: (b -2h, 2b -17 +h)=(41 -32, 82 -17 +16)=(9, 81). Since y=81, and x=9, which is on the parabola y=x\u00b2 (9\u00b2=81). Good.\n\nPoint D: (a -2h, 2a -17 +h)=(25 -32, 50 -17 +16)=(-7, 49). y=49, x=-7, (-7)^2=49. So that's also on the parabola. So both points are on the parabola. So h=16 is valid.\n\nSimilarly, h=4:\n\na=(3*4 +2)/2=(12 +2)/2=14/2=7\n\nb=7 +4=11\n\nPoint C: (11 -8, 22 -17 +4)=(3, 9). y=9, x=3, 3\u00b2=9. Good.\n\nPoint D: (7 -8,14 -17 +4)=(-1,1). y=1, x=-1, (-1)^2=1. Also good. So both h=16 and h=4 are valid.\n\nTherefore, the possible side lengths are h\u221a5. For h=16, the area is (16\u221a5)^2=256*5=1280. For h=4, the area is (4\u221a5)^2=16*5=80.\n\nSo, the minimum possible area is 80.\n\nWait, but hold on. The problem says \"the minimum possible area of the square\". So between 80 and 1280, 80 is smaller. Therefore, 80 is the answer. But let me just verify if there are other possibilities. Because we assumed that the displacement from B to C is (-2h, h). What if it's the other direction, (2h, -h)? Then points C and D would be below the line y=2x -17, but since the parabola is above the line, those points can't lie on the parabola. Hence, only the direction upwards is valid. Therefore, only h=4 and h=16 are possible.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm that h=4 and h=16 correspond to different positions of the square. When h=4, the side length is 4\u221a5, area 80. When h=16, side length 16\u221a5, area 1280. So 80 is the minimum. Therefore, the answer is 80.\n\nBut let me double-check the calculations. For h=4, a=7, so point A is (7, 2*7 -17)=(7, 14 -17)=(7, -3). Point B is (7+4, 2*(7+4)-17)=(11, 22 -17)=(11,5). Then, point C is (11 -8,5 +4)=(3,9), which is on the parabola. Point D is (7 -8, -3 +4)=(-1,1), which is on the parabola. The distance AB: between (7,-3) and (11,5). The difference is (4,8), so length sqrt(16 +64)=sqrt(80)=4\u221a5. Then BC is from (11,5) to (3,9). Difference (-8,4), length sqrt(64 +16)=sqrt(80)=4\u221a5. So that's a square. Similarly, the other one with h=16 gives a larger square. So 80 is indeed the minimum.\n\nBut wait, is there a possibility of other squares with different orientations? Because squares can be placed such that the side is not horizontal or vertical, but here it's constrained to have a side on the line y=2x -17. So the square must have one side along this line, which has slope 2. Therefore, the square is rotated such that one side is along that line. The other sides are perpendicular. So as per the problem, there are only two possible squares? Or maybe more?\n\nBut according to our equations, we found two solutions. But perhaps there are more? Let me think. When we set up the problem, we considered points A and B on the line, then moving in the direction perpendicular to the line to reach points C and D on the parabola. Depending on the direction (left or right), but since the parabola is symmetric, but the line is straight, perhaps there are only two such squares. However, in our calculation, we found two solutions. However, in reality, when h=4, the square is smaller, and when h=16, it's larger. So 80 is the minimum.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a way to parameterize the square differently. Let me try another approach. Let's suppose that the square has side length s. The direction along the line y=2x -17 is given by the vector (1,2), so the side vector can be written as t*(1,2), where t is a scalar. The length of this vector is t*sqrt(1 +4)=t*sqrt(5), so s = t*sqrt(5). Therefore, t = s/sqrt(5).\n\nThen, the perpendicular direction would be vectors (-2,1) as before, since perpendicular to (1,2). So moving from point B by a vector proportional to (-2,1) to reach point C. Since the length of this vector should also be s, so the vector would be ( -2s/sqrt(5), s/sqrt(5) ). Because the vector (-2,1) has length sqrt(4 +1)=sqrt(5), so scaling it by s/sqrt(5) gives length s.\n\nTherefore, if point A is (a, 2a -17), point B is (a + t, 2a +2t -17). Then point C is (a + t - 2s/sqrt(5), 2a +2t -17 + s/sqrt(5)). Similarly, point D is (a - 2s/sqrt(5), 2a -17 + s/sqrt(5)).\n\nSince points C and D are on the parabola y = x\u00b2, their coordinates must satisfy this. Therefore:\n\nFor point C:\n\n2a +2t -17 + s/sqrt(5) = [a + t - 2s/sqrt(5)]\u00b2\n\nFor point D:\n\n2a -17 + s/sqrt(5) = [a - 2s/sqrt(5)]\u00b2\n\nBut since s = t*sqrt(5), we can substitute s = t*sqrt(5). Then, s/sqrt(5) = t, and 2s/sqrt(5) = 2t.\n\nTherefore, substituting s = t*sqrt(5):\n\nFor point C:\n\n2a +2t -17 + t = [a + t - 2t]\u00b2 => 2a +3t -17 = (a - t)^2\n\nFor point D:\n\n2a -17 + t = (a -2t)^2\n\nSo we have:\n\nEquation (1): (a - t)^2 = 2a +3t -17\n\nEquation (2): (a -2t)^2 = 2a + t -17\n\nThese are the same equations as before, with h = t. Wait, yes, h was the horizontal difference between a and b, which was t. So this is the same system. Therefore, solving these equations as before gives the same solutions. Therefore, the minimal area is 80.\n\nBut just to ensure that there's no smaller square, maybe we can consider if the square is placed such that not the entire side is on the line, but only part of it? Wait, no, the problem states \"one side of square ABCD lies on the line\", meaning the entire side is on the line, so two vertices are on the line, and the other two are on the parabola. So our previous reasoning holds.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different orientation? But since the square must have a side on the line y=2x -17, the orientation is fixed by that line's slope. Therefore, the square is uniquely determined by the position along the line and the side length. Hence, the two solutions we found are the only possible ones, and 80 is indeed the minimal area.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible area of the square is 80.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe minimum possible area of the square is \\boxed{80}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given a square \\(ABCD\\) where one side lies on the line \\(y = 2x - 17\\) and the other two vertices are on the parabola \\(y = x^2\\), we need to find the minimum possible area of the square.\n\n1. **Identify Points on the Line and Parabola**:\n   - Let points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be on the line \\(y = 2x - 17\\) with coordinates \\(A(a, 2a - 17)\\) and \\(B(b, 2b - 17)\\).\n   - Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) must be on the parabola \\(y = x^2\\).\n\n2. **Vectors and Perpendicularity**:\n   - The vector \\(\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) is \\((b - a, 2(b - a))\\).\n   - The length of \\(\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) is \\(h\\sqrt{5}\\) where \\(h = b - a\\).\n   - The perpendicular vector to \\(\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) is \\((-2h, h)\\).\n\n3. **Coordinates of Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)**:\n   - Point \\(C\\) coordinates: \\((b - 2h, 2b - 17 + h)\\)\n   - Point \\(D\\) coordinates: \\((a - 2h, 2a - 17 + h)\\)\n\n4. **Substitute into Parabola Equation**:\n   - For point \\(C\\): \\(2b - 17 + h = (b - 2h)^2\\)\n   - For point \\(D\\): \\(2a - 17 + h = (a - 2h)^2\\)\n\n5. **Solve the System of Equations**:\n   - Substitute \\(b = a + h\\) into the equations.\n   - Derive two equations and solve for \\(a\\) and \\(h\\):\n     - \\(2a + 3h - 17 = (a - h)^2\\)\n     - \\(2a + h - 17 = (a - 2h)^2\\)\n\n6. **Find Solutions**:\n   - Solving the quadratic equations yields \\(h = 4\\) or \\(h = 16\\).\n   - Corresponding side lengths are \\(4\\sqrt{5}\\) and \\(16\\sqrt{5}\\).\n\n7. **Calculate Areas**:\n   - Area for \\(h = 4\\): \\((4\\sqrt{5})^2 = 80\\)\n   - Area for \\(h = 16\\): \\((16\\sqrt{5})^2 = 1280\\)\n\nThe minimum possible area of the square is \\(\\boxed{80}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the problem details**: We are given that one side of the square $ABCD$ lies on the line \\( y = 2x - 17 \\), while the other two vertices lie on the parabola \\( y = x^2 \\). We need to find the minimum area of the square.\n\n2. **Equation of the line passing through vertices \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)**: Suppose the coordinates of vertices \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the parabola are \\( C\\left(x_1, y_1\\right) \\) and \\( D\\left(x_2, y_2\\right) \\). Since these points must lie on a line with the equation \\( y = 2x + b \\), substituting into the parabola equation we get:\n   \\[\n   y = x^2 \\implies  x^{2} = 2x + b\n   \\]\n   Solving this quadratic equation we find the \\( x \\)-coordinates of points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\):\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 2x - b = 0 \\implies x_{1,2} = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + b}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Length of each side of the square**: Let the side length of the square be \\( a \\). Then:\n   \\[\n   a^{2} = \\left(x_{1} - x_{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(y_{1} - y_{2}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the coordinates \\( x_{1} = 1 + \\sqrt{1 + b} \\) and \\( x_{2} = 1 - \\sqrt{1 + b} \\), and the corresponding \\( y \\)-coordinates:\n\n   \\[\n   x_1 - x_2 = 2\\sqrt{1 + b}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   y_1 = (x_1)^2 = (1 + \\sqrt{1 + b})^2 = 1 + 2\\sqrt{1 + b} + (1 + b)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y_2 = (x_2)^2 = (1 - \\sqrt{1 + b})^2 = 1 - 2\\sqrt{1 + b} + (1 + b)\n   \\]\n   \n   Now, \n   \\[\n   y_1 - y_2 = 4 \\sqrt{1 + b}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\left( y_1 - y_2 \\right)^2 = 16(1 + b)\n   \\]\n\n   So,\n   \\[\n   a^2 = \\left(x_{1} - x_{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(y_{1} - y_{2}\\right)^2 = 4(1 + b) + 16(1 + b) = 20(1 + b)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Choosing a point on the line \\( y = 2x - 17 \\) and computing the distance**: Let's choose the point \\((6, -5)\\) on the line \\( y = 2x - 17 \\). The distance from this point \\((6, -5)\\) to the line \\( y = 2x + b \\) is equal to the side length of the square, \\( a \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = a = \\frac{|2(6) - (-5) + b|}{\\sqrt{1^2 + 2^2}} = \\frac{|17 + b|}{\\sqrt{5}}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Setting up the equation and solving for \\( b \\)**: Using the two expressions for \\( a \\) from the given line \\( y=2x-17 \\) and our derived formula:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{17 + b}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\sqrt{ 20(1 + b)}\n   \\]\n   Square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{17 + b}{\\sqrt{5}} \\right)^2 = 20(1 + b) \\implies \\frac{(17 + b)^2}{5} = 20(1 + b)\n   \\]\n   Multiply both sides by 5:\n   \\[\n   (17 + b)^2 = 100(1 + b)\n   \\]\n   Expand and solve the quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   289 + 34b + b^2 = 100 + 100b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b^2 - 66b + 189 = 0\n   \\]\n   Using the quadratic formula \\( b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A} \\):\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{66 \\pm \\sqrt{4356 - 756}}{2} = \\frac{66 \\pm \\sqrt{3600}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{66 \\pm 60}{2}\n   \\]\n   This results in:\n   \\[\n   b_1 = \\frac{126}{2} = 63 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad  b_2 = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n   \\]\n\n6. **Finding the minimum area**: Substituting \\( b_1 \\) and \\( b_2 \\) back into \\( a^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   a^2 = 20(1 + 63) = 1280 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad a^2 = 20(1 + 3) = 80\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the minimum area of the square is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{80}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In the given multiplication, different letters represent different integers whose possible values are $2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9$.\n\nG8.1 Find $A$.  \nG8.2 Find $B$.  \nG8.3 Find $C$.  \nG8.4 Find $D$.  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrrr} \n& 1 & A & B & C & D & E \\\\\n\\times & & & & & & 3 \\\\\n\\hline & A & B & C & D & E & 1 \\\\\n\\hline & 1 & A & B & C & D & 7 \\\\\n\\times & & & & & & 3 \\\\\n\\hline & A & B & C & D & 7 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline & 1 & A & B & C & 5 & 7 \\\\\n\\times & & & & & & 3 \\\\\n\\hline & A & B & C & 5 & 7 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline & 1 & A & B