le on one of the legs. So the circle is inside the triangle, touching the hypotenuse at the midpoint, and the question is how long is the chord where the circle intersects one of the legs.\n\nTo find this, perhaps I can model the triangle and the circle in a coordinate system. Let me try that.\n\nLet's place the right angle at the origin (0,0), one leg along the x-axis, and the other along the y-axis. So the vertices are at (0,0), (20\u221a2, 0), and (0, 20\u221a2). The hypotenuse is the line connecting (20\u221a2, 0) and (0, 20\u221a2). The midpoint of the hypotenuse is at (10\u221a2, 10\u221a2). The altitude from the right angle (0,0) to the hypotenuse is the line y = x, since the hypotenuse has slope -1, so the perpendicular slope is 1. The altitude meets the hypotenuse at its midpoint (10\u221a2, 10\u221a2). Therefore, the center of the circle is along this altitude (y = x line), 9 units away from the hypotenuse towards the origin. Wait, but how to find the coordinates of the center?\n\nAlternatively, since the distance from the hypotenuse to the center is 9, and the altitude is 20, the center is 9 units from the hypotenuse along the altitude. So starting from the midpoint (10\u221a2, 10\u221a2), moving towards the origin along y = x. The direction vector from the midpoint to the origin is (-10\u221a2, -10\u221a2). The length of this vector is sqrt( (10\u221a2)^2 + (10\u221a2)^2 ) = sqrt(200 + 200) = sqrt(400) = 20. So to move 9 units along this direction from the midpoint, we can parameterize it.\n\nThe unit vector in the direction from midpoint to origin is (-10\u221a2/20, -10\u221a2/20) = (-\u221a2/2, -\u221a2/2). So moving 9 units along this direction from the midpoint gives the center coordinates as:\n\n(10\u221a2 + (-\u221a2/2)*9, 10\u221a2 + (-\u221a2/2)*9) = (10\u221a2 - (9\u221a2)/2, 10\u221a2 - (9\u221a2)/2) = ( (20\u221a2 - 9\u221a2)/2, same for y ) = (11\u221a2/2, 11\u221a2/2). So center at (11\u221a2/2, 11\u221a2/2).\n\nWait, let's verify that. The 