ight is equivalent to splitting N into two parts: the higher part (all digits except the last two) and the lower part (the last two digits). Then inserting a 0 between them.\n\nSo, if N is written as A * 100 + B, where B is the last two digits (since we're inserting between the second and third digits from the right), then inserting a 0 would result in A * 1000 + 0 * 100 + B = A * 1000 + B.\n\nAccording to the problem, this new number is equal to 9N. Therefore:\n\nA * 1000 + B = 9 * (A * 100 + B)\n\nLet me write that equation:\n\n1000A + B = 9(100A + B)\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\n1000A + B = 900A + 9B\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\n1000A + B - 900A - 9B = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n100A - 8B = 0\n\nSo,\n\n100A = 8B\n\nSimplify:\n\n25A = 2B\n\nTherefore,\n\n25 divides 2B. Since 25 and 2 are coprime, 25 divides B. So B must be a two-digit number divisible by 25. The possible values for B are 00, 25, 50, 75. However, since B is the last two digits of N, which is a natural number, B can be 00 only if N ends with two zeros, but then N would have at least two trailing zeros, but inserting a 0 between the second and third digits from the right would still result in a number with two trailing zeros. However, if B is 00, then 25A = 2*00 = 0, so A must be 0. But A is the part of N before the last two digits, so if A is 0, then N is 00, which isn't a natural number. Therefore, B cannot be 00. So possible Bs are 25, 50, 75.\n\nNow, substituting these possible Bs into the equation 25A = 2B.\n\nCase 1: B = 25\n\n25A = 2*25 = 50\n\nThus, A = 50 / 25 = 2\n\nTherefore, N = A*100 + B = 2*100 + 25 = 200 + 25 = 225\n\nCheck: Inserting a 0 between the second and third digits from the right in 225. Let's see. The digits of 225 are 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (units). Inserting a 0 between the second and third digits from the right (tens and hundreds place) would mean inserting between the 2 (tens) and 2 (hundreds). Wait, hold on. Wait, the digits from the right are 5 (units), 2 (tens)