e labels must be equal:\n   \\[\n   a_1 = a_2 = \\ldots = a_{99}.\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{All \\text{ labels must be equal}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) be positive real constants. For each real number \\(t\\), let \\((x_t, y_t)\\) be the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola \\(y = ax^2 + tx + c\\). Then the shape formed on the plane by the vertices \\((x_t, y_t)\\) for all real numbers \\(t\\) is:\n(A) A straight line.\n(B) A parabola.\n(C) A part of a parabola, but not the whole parabola.\n(D) One branch of a hyperbola.\n(E) None of the above.\n\n(35th American High School Mathematics Examination, 1984)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here about parabolas and their vertices. Let me try to understand what it's asking. The question says that for each real number t, there's a parabola given by the equation y = ax\u00b2 + tx + c, where a and c are positive constants. The vertex of each such parabola is (x_t, y_t). They want to know what shape is formed by all these vertices as t varies over all real numbers. The options are a straight line, a parabola, part of a parabola (but not the whole thing), one branch of a hyperbola, or none of the above. Hmm.\n\nFirst, I need to recall how to find the vertex of a parabola given by an equation in standard form. The standard form is usually y = ax\u00b2 + bx + c, and the vertex (h, k) can be found using h = -b/(2a) and then plugging back into the equation to find k. In this case, the equation given is y = ax\u00b2 + tx + c. So here, the coefficient of x is t, which is varying. So for each t, the coefficient of x changes, which will change the vertex.\n\nLet me write down the vertex coordinates for a general t. The standard formula for the x-coordinate of the vertex is -b/(2a). Here, the quadratic coefficient is a, the linear coefficient is t, and the constant term is c. So the x-coordinate