bined terms:\n   - The \\(\\cos^{2} \\alpha \\cos^{2} \\varphi\\) terms: \\((-1 + 2)\\cos^{2} \\alpha \\cos^{2} \\varphi = \\cos^{2} \\alpha \\cos^{2} \\varphi\\)\n   - The cross terms \\(-2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\varphi\\) and \\(2 \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\varphi \\sin \\varphi\\) cancel each other out.\n   - The remaining terms: \\(\\sin^{2} \\varphi - \\sin^{2} \\alpha \\sin^{2} \\varphi + \\cos^{2} \\alpha \\cos^{2} \\varphi\\)\n\n5. Factor out common terms:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^{2} \\varphi (1 - \\sin^{2} \\alpha) + \\cos^{2} \\alpha \\cos^{2} \\varphi\n   \\]\n   Using the Pythagorean identity \\(1 - \\sin^{2} \\alpha = \\cos^{2} \\alpha\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin^{2} \\varphi \\cos^{2} \\alpha + \\cos^{2} \\alpha \\cos^{2} \\varphi = \\cos^{2} \\alpha (\\sin^{2} \\varphi + \\cos^{2} \\varphi) = \\cos^{2} \\alpha\n   \\]\n\nThus, the left-hand side simplifies to \\(\\cos^{2} \\alpha\\), confirming the identity. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\cos^{2} \\alpha}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Begin with the given expression:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\varphi - \\cos^2 (\\alpha - \\varphi) + 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi \\cos (\\alpha - \\varphi)\n   \\]\n\n2. Expand the term $- \\cos^2 (\\alpha - \\varphi)$ using the trigonometric identity for cosine:\n   \\[\n   \\cos(\\alpha - \\varphi) = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\varphi\n   \\]\n   Hence, \n   \\[\n   \\cos^2(\\alpha - \\varphi) = (\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\varphi)^2\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute and simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\varphi - \\left( \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\varphi \\right)^2 + 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi (\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\varphi)\n   \\]\n\n4. Expand the squared term:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\varphi \\right)^2 = \\cos^2 \\alpha \\cos^2 \\varphi + 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\varphi \\sin \\alpha \