n     Thus, \\( T(144) < T(143) \\).\n\nSince the ratio \\( \\frac{T(143)}{T(142)} \\) is slightly greater than 1 and \\( \\frac{T(144)}{T(143)} \\) is less than 1, the maximum term occurs at \\( k = 143 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{143}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Step 1**: Expand the given expression \\( (1 + \\sqrt{5})^{206} \\) using the Binomial Theorem.\n\n   The Binomial Theorem states that for any positive integer \\( n \\) and any numbers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\),\n   \\[\n   (a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k\n   \\]\n   Applying this to our expression:\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\sqrt{5})^{206} = \\sum_{k=0}^{206} \\binom{206}{k} 1^{206-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k\n   \\]\n   Since \\( 1^{206-k} = 1 \\) for all values of \\( k \\), we can simplify it further:\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\sqrt{5})^{206} = \\sum_{k=0}^{206} \\binom{206}{k} (\\sqrt{5})^k\n   \\]\n\n2. **Step 2**: Focus on the term \\(\\binom{206}{k} (\\sqrt{5})^k\\), and determine which value of \\( k \\) maximizes this term.\n   \n3. **Step 3**: Consider the ratio of consecutive terms in the binomial expansion:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\binom{206}{k+1} (\\sqrt{5})^{k+1}}{\\binom{206}{k} (\\sqrt{5})^k} = \\frac{\\binom{206}{k+1}}{\\binom{206}{k}} \\sqrt{5}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\binom{206}{k+1} = \\frac{206!}{(k+1)!(206-(k+1))!}\\) and \\(\\binom{206}{k} = \\frac{206!}{k!(206-k)!}\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\binom{206}{k+1}}{\\binom{206}{k}} = \\frac{(206-k)}{(k+1)}\n   \\]\n   Substituting this back, the ratio becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{206-k}{k+1} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Step 4**: Set the ratio greater than 1 to find the maximum term:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{206-k}{k+1} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} > 1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the inequality:\n   \\[\n   (206 - k) \\sqrt{5} > k + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   206 \\sqrt{5} - k \\sqrt{5} > k + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   206 \\sqrt{5} - 1 > k (\\sqrt{5} + 1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k < \\frac{206 \\sqrt{5} - 1}{\\sqrt{5} + 1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Step 5**: Calculate the numerical 