ty failing.\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible a is 2, so the range is a \u22642.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe range of the real number \\( a \\) is \\boxed{(-\\infty, 2]}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 2x + a \\cdot \\ln x \\), we need to find the range of the real number \\( a \\) such that the inequality \\( f(2t - 1) \\geq 2f(t) - 3 \\) holds for all \\( t \\geq 1 \\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( x = 2t - 1 \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[\nf(2t - 1) = (2t - 1)^2 + 2(2t - 1) + a \\cdot \\ln(2t - 1)\n\\]\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\\[\nf(2t - 1) = 4t^2 - 4t + 1 + 4t - 2 + a \\cdot \\ln(2t - 1) = 4t^2 - 1 + a \\cdot \\ln(2t - 1)\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute \\( 2f(t) - 3 \\):\n\\[\n2f(t) - 3 = 2(t^2 + 2t + a \\cdot \\ln t) - 3 = 2t^2 + 4t + 2a \\cdot \\ln t - 3\n\\]\n\nWe then substitute these expressions into the inequality \\( f(2t - 1) \\geq 2f(t) - 3 \\):\n\\[\n4t^2 - 1 + a \\cdot \\ln(2t - 1) \\geq 2t^2 + 4t + 2a \\cdot \\ln t - 3\n\\]\nSimplifying both sides:\n\\[\n2t^2 - 4t + 2 + a \\left( \\ln(2t - 1) - 2 \\ln t \\right) \\geq 0\n\\]\nRewriting the logarithmic term:\n\\[\n2t^2 - 4t + 2 + a \\cdot \\ln\\left( \\frac{2t - 1}{t^2} \\right) \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nWe analyze the logarithmic term \\( \\ln\\left( \\frac{2t - 1}{t^2} \\right) \\), which is negative for \\( t > 1 \\). Rearranging the inequality to isolate \\( a \\):\n\\[\na \\cdot \\ln\\left( \\frac{2t - 1}{t^2} \\right) \\geq - (2t^2 - 4t + 2)\n\\]\nSince the coefficient of \\( a \\) is negative, we divide both sides by it, reversing the inequality:\n\\[\na \\leq \\frac{2t^2 - 4t + 2}{\\ln\\left( \\frac{t^2}{2t - 1} \\right)}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the minimum value of the right-hand side for \\( t \\geq 1 \\). Analyzing the function \\( h(t) = \\frac{2t^2 - 4t + 2}{\\ln\\left( \\frac{t^2}{2t - 1} \\right)} \\), we find that as \\( t \\) approaches 1 from the right, \\( h(t) \\) approaches 2, and as \\( t \\) approaches infinity, \\( h(t) \\) tends to infinity. Therefore, the minimum 