rees. Since AB is the vector from B to A (or A to B?), wait, angle at B between AB and BN. Wait, AB is the side from A to B, so the vector AB is (x, y) since A is (0,0) and B is (x,y). Then BN is the vector from B to N, which is (c - x, -y). The angle between vectors AB and BN is 50 degrees.\n\nWait, the angle at point B between BA and BN is 50 degrees. Wait, but vectors BA and BN. BA is from B to A, which is (-x, -y), and BN is from B to N, which is (c - x, -y). The angle between BA and BN is 50 degrees. So, using the dot product formula:\n\ncos\u03b8 = (BA \u00b7 BN) / (|BA| |BN|)\n\nSo:\n\ncos(50\u00b0) = [(-x)(c - x) + (-y)(-y)] / [\u221a(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2) * \u221a((c - x)\u00b2 + y\u00b2)]\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n(-x(c - x) + y\u00b2) = -cx + x\u00b2 + y\u00b2\n\nDenominator:\n\n\u221a(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2) * \u221a((c - x)\u00b2 + y\u00b2) = |BA| * |BN| = AB * BN\n\nBut we know that AB = 2 BN, so AB = 2 BN. So, BN = AB / 2. Therefore, the denominator becomes AB * (AB / 2) = (AB\u00b2)/2.\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\ncos(50\u00b0) = (-cx + x\u00b2 + y\u00b2) / ( (AB\u00b2)/2 )\n\nBut AB\u00b2 is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2. So:\n\ncos(50\u00b0) = 2(-cx + x\u00b2 + y\u00b2) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2)\n\nLet me write this as:\n\ncos(50\u00b0) = 2( x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 )\n\nSimplify the right side:\n\n2( x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 ) = 2 - (2 c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore:\n\ncos(50\u00b0) = 2 - (2 c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 )\n\nRearranging:\n\n(2 c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 ) = 2 - cos(50\u00b0)\n\nSo:\n\n(2 c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 ) = 2 - cos(50\u00b0)\n\nLet me compute 2 - cos(50\u00b0). Cos(50\u00b0) is approximately 0.6428, so 2 - 0.6428 \u2248 1.3572. But let's keep it exact for now.\n\nSo, (2 c x ) / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 ) \u2248 1.3572\n\nBut we also have from the first equation AB = 2 BN:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = 4[(x - c)^2 + y\u00b2]\n\nWhich we had simplified to:\n\n3x\u00b2 - 8c x + 4c\u00b2 + 3y\u00b