nded by the lines \\(3x + y = 6\\) and \\(3x + y = -6\\).\n\n2. **Find intersections of boundary lines**:\n\n   We solve for the intersections of these lines to find the vertices of the parallelogram:\n   - Solve for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) by setting \\(x - y = 2\\) and \\(3x + y = 6\\):\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n       x - y = 2 \\\\\n       3x + y = 6\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Adding these two equations:\n     \\[\n     x - y + 3x + y = 2 + 6 \\implies 4x = 8 \\implies x = 2\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x = 2\\) back into \\(x - y = 2\\):\n     \\[\n     2 - y = 2 \\implies y = 0\n     \\]\n     So one vertex is \\((2, 0)\\).\n\n   - Solve for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) by setting \\(x - y = 2\\) and \\(3x + y = -6\\):\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n       x - y = 2 \\\\\n       3x + y = -6\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Adding these two equations:\n     \\[\n     x - y + 3x + y = 2 - 6 \\implies 4x = -4 \\implies x = -1\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x = -1\\) back into \\(x - y = 2\\):\n     \\[\n     -1 - y = 2 \\implies y = -3\n     \\]\n     So another vertex is \\((-1, -3)\\).\n\n   - Solve for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) by setting \\(x - y = -2\\) and \\(3x + y = 6\\):\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n       x - y = -2 \\\\\n       3x + y = 6\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Adding these two equations:\n     \\[\n     x - y + 3x + y = -2 + 6 \\implies 4x = 4 \\implies x = 1\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x = 1\\) back into \\(x - y = -2\\):\n     \\[\n     1 - y = -2 \\implies y = 3\n     \\]\n     So another vertex is \\((1, 3)\\).\n\n   - Solve for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) by setting \\(x - y = -2\\) and \\(3x + y = -6\\):\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n       x - y = -2 \\\\\n       3x + y = -6\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Adding these two equations:\n     \\[\n     x - y + 3x + y = -2 - 6 \\implies 4x = -8 \\implies x = -2\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x = -2\\) back into \\(x - y = -2\\):\n     \\[\n     -2 - y = -2 \\implies y = 0\n     \