and \\(B\\):\n\n1. **Simplify the equation for set \\(A\\)**:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y-3}{x-1} = 2\n   \\]\n   Multiplying both sides by \\(x-1\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   y - 3 = 2(x - 1)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   y - 3 = 2x - 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + 1\n   \\]\n   Hence, the set \\(A\\) is the line defined by \\(y = 2x + 1\\), excluding the point where \\(x = 1\\) (i.e., the point \\((1, 3)\\)). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   A = \\{(x, y) \\mid y = 2x + 1, \\, x \\ne 1\\}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify conditions for set \\(B\\)**:\n   \\[\n   4x + ay = 16\n   \\]\n   For \\(A \\cap B = \\varnothing\\), there are two cases to examine:\n   - The lines are parallel and do not intersect.\n   - The lines intersect at the point \\((1, 3)\\) which must be excluded from \\(A\\).\n\n3. **Case 1: Lines are parallel**:\n   \n   For the lines to be parallel, their slopes must be equal. The slope of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) is \\(2\\).\n\n   The slope of the line \\(4x + ay = 16\\) can be found by rewriting it in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + c\\). First, solve for \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   ay = -4x + 16\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{4}{a}x + \\frac{16}{a}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the slope \\(m\\) is:\n   \\[\n   m = -\\frac{4}{a}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the slopes must be equal, we have:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{4}{a} = 2\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   -4 = 2a\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a = -2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Case 2: Lines intersect at the point \\((1, 3)\\)**:\n   \n   If the lines intersect at the point \\((1, 3)\\), it means that this point satisfies the equation of line \\(B\\). Substitute \\(x = 1\\) and \\(y = 3\\) into \\(4x + ay = 16\\):\n   \\[\n   4(1) + 3a = 16\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   4 + 3a = 16\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3a = 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a = 4\n   \\]\n\nConclusively, the possible values of \\(a\\) are \\(-2\\) and \\(4\\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2 \\text{ or } 4}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code"